<%#
  Comments on this file as a whole (GitHub `subjectType: FILE`), at the top of
  its section where they set the frame for reading it.

  They have no line, so there is no block to sit under. Prism's own file-level
  fallback — a comment on a block GitHub can't anchor to — lands here too:
  ReviewCommentsController streams it into this file's container, which is why
  the container is keyed by path and carries `data-file-threads-for`. The
  heading is always in the DOM so a first streamed-in thread gets one; the
  section hides itself while the list is empty.

  Locals: threads ([Github::Types::ReviewThread]), page (Review::Page),
          pull_request
%>
<%# The section hides itself while its list is empty, in one self-contained
    rule rather than the stylesheet's `.file-threads`: that one is written
    against `#file_threads`, a page-wide id that cannot exist now there is one
    of these per file. ws-darkmode owns the stylesheet and has the request. %>
<section class="md-row [&:not(:has(.file-threads-list>*))]:hidden"
         data-testid="file-threads-section"
         aria-labelledby="file-threads-heading-<%= page.file_key %>">
  <div class="md-gutter" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <div class="md-body">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h3 class="section-title" id="file-threads-heading-<%= page.file_key %>">Comments on this file</h3>
    </div>

    <div id="<%= file_threads_dom_id(page.path) %>" class="file-threads-list"
         data-file-threads-for="<%= page.path %>" data-testid="file-threads">
      <% threads.each do |thread| %>
        <%= render "thread", thread: thread, pull_request: pull_request, block_id: "file" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
